const faq = [
  { q: 'What is Samjok-o?', a: 'Samjok-o (삼족오, literally "three-legged crow") is a mythological three-legged corvid that inhabits the sun in Korean and broader East Asian mythology. In Korean tradition, the Samjok-o appears most prominently in murals of the Goguryeo kingdom (37 BCE–668 CE) — particularly in royal tomb paintings — where it is depicted as a large three-legged bird inside a solar disc. The three legs are interpreted as representing the three phases of the sun (dawn, noon, dusk) or the three-part cosmos (heaven, earth, underworld). The Samjok-o is a symbol of royal power and divine authority.' },
  { q: 'Is Samjok-o a raven or a crow?', a: 'The Korean term for Samjok-o uses the character for "crow" (오, O), but the mythological bird is not specifically identified as either a crow (Corvus corone or similar) or a raven (Corvus corax) in the ornithological sense. The three-legged solar corvid is a mythological archetype found across East Asia — in Chinese mythology it is the Sanzuwu (三足烏); in Japanese mythology, the Yatagarasu (八咫烏) is a related three-legged crow. These figures share the solar association but differ in their specific legendary roles. Korean scholarly usage typically refers to the Samjok-o as a "crow" (까마귀, kkamagwi), but the distinction between crow and raven is not culturally significant in this mythological context.' },
  { q: 'How does Korean raven mythology compare to Japanese?', a: 'The Korean Samjok-o and the Japanese Yatagarasu are closely related mythological figures, both deriving from the Chinese Sanzuwu solar-bird tradition. The Yatagarasu is more fully elaborated in surviving Japanese mythological literature — it appears in the Nihon Shoki (720 CE) as a divine guide sent by the gods to lead Emperor Jimmu — while the Samjok-o survives primarily through Goguryeo tomb art rather than extensive textual elaboration. Both traditions associate the three-legged corvid with the sun, with guidance, and with divine authority. Korean folk tradition also includes negative corvid associations (the crow as an ill-omen bird, kkamagwi, whose cry predicts misfortune) alongside the positive solar Samjok-o — a duality paralleling the ambivalent raven symbolism in many world traditions.' },
];
