    <h1>Ravens and Crows in Korean Mythology: Samjok-o and the Solar Bird</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Samjok-o — the three-legged solar corvid of Korean mythology — inhabits the sun and embodies divine authority. Depicted in Goguryeo royal tomb murals (1st century BCE–7th century CE), it is East Asia's most explicit fusion of corvid and solar symbolism: the black bird that lives inside light. This tradition places Korean corvid mythology within a broader East Asian solar-bird complex that includes the Chinese Sanzuwu and the Japanese Yatagarasu.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Samjok-o in Goguryeo Tomb Art</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The primary evidence for the Samjok-o in Korean tradition comes from Goguryeo tomb murals, particularly royal tombs in the Pyongyang and Ji'an regions:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The Three-Legged Tomb (삼족오총, Samjok-o Chong) and the Muyong Chong (Dance Tomb) contain ceiling murals depicting a three-legged bird inside a solar disc — typically a red or gold circle representing the sun.</li>
      <li>The bird is shown with clearly three legs (a visual anomaly that marks it as supernatural), a large body, and sometimes outstretched wings. It is typically paired with a toad or hare in a lunar disc — the solar and lunar animals framing the cosmos.</li>
      <li>The placement on tomb ceilings — representing the heavens — positions the Samjok-o as a celestial being that guards and guides the deceased in their passage to the afterlife.</li>
      <li>Goguryeo tomb murals are UNESCO-listed world heritage sites; the Samjok-o appears in dozens of surviving examples, making it one of the most consistently depicted mythological animals in Korean art.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Three-Legged Bird Across East Asia</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Samjok-o belongs to a wider East Asian solar-corvid tradition:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chinese Sanzuwu (三足烏):</strong> the Chinese three-legged solar bird appears in Zhou dynasty (1046–256 BCE) texts and Han dynasty tomb art. The Chinese mythological framework (ten suns, each inhabited by a corvid) provides the textual elaboration that the Korean tradition largely lacks.</li>
      <li><strong>Japanese Yatagarasu (八咫烏):</strong> the "eight-span crow" sent by the sun goddess Amaterasu to guide Emperor Jimmu — a guiding divine bird rather than a solar inhabitant, but sharing the three legs and corvid form.</li>
      <li>The shared feature of three legs is interpreted in various ways across traditions: the three phases of the daily sun; the three-layer cosmos; or simply as a marker of supernatural status (normal birds have two legs; gods' birds have three).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Corvids in Korean Folk Tradition</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Alongside the elevated solar Samjok-o, corvids (crows, kkamagwi, 까마귀) carry negative folk associations in Korean popular tradition:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The cry of a crow near a house is traditionally interpreted as a death omen or ill fortune — a belief widespread across Korea and shared with Japanese and Chinese folk belief.</li>
      <li>Corvids are associated with mourning and funerals in Korean folk practice — their black plumage and carrion-feeding habits aligning them with death in the folk imagination even while the mythological Samjok-o is a solar king.</li>
      <li>This duality — the divine solar corvid versus the ill-omen folk crow — mirrors the ambivalent status of corvids in many world traditions, where the same bird simultaneously symbolises otherworldly power and earthly bad luck.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

